slurm-monitor: honour resolution_in_s on every query

time_bucket() is compiled with its width as a literal in the SQL text, but
was marked inherit_cache = True. SQLAlchemy then caches the compiled
statement by shape and reuses the first width for the life of the process:
after one query at 257 s, a query for 60 s still returns 257 s buckets.
Marking the function not cacheable makes each query use its own width.

diff --git a/src/slurm_monitor/timescaledb/functions.py b/src/slurm_monitor/timescaledb/functions.py
index eee2122..9cc19c2 100644
--- a/src/slurm_monitor/timescaledb/functions.py
+++ b/src/slurm_monitor/timescaledb/functions.py
@@ -18,7 +18,10 @@ class time_bucket(GenericFunction):
     identifier = "time_bucket"
 
     type = TIMESTAMP()
-    inherit_cache = True
+    # The bucket width is written into the SQL text as a literal (see the
+    # compilers below), so a cached compilation would carry the first width
+    # into every later query of the same shape. Not cacheable.
+    inherit_cache = False
 
 
 # For TimeScaledb
